WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.040
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Stackdriver
is a great platform

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.040
that lets developers and
operators measure and improve

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.820
service reliability.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.400
However, collecting
a lot of data

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.800
will make it hard to find
the information you need.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.510
And you may be paying too much.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.880
Today we'll get into
understanding and managing

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.460
your logging and
monitoring costs.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.480
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.630
Over the last two
episodes, we reviewed

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.580
using OpenCensus and
Prometheus to add

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.310
custom metric instrumentation
to your services.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.710
But what is that going to
mean for your cloud spend?

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.080
Let's start by reviewing how
Stackdriver monitoring is

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.010
priced.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.385
This is the current pricing.

00:00:39.385 --> 00:00:41.010
But make sure to
check the pricing page

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.630
link below for
up-to-date information.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.630
The first thing to
understand is that monitoring

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.030
is charged by the
volume of data required

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.820
to store monitoring metrics
that aren't generated

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.510
by GCP services.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.640
This means that metrics from
the monitoring agent or custom

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.830
metrics sent by instrumentation
like OpenCensus or Prometheus

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.738
will result in charges.

00:01:00.738 --> 00:01:02.280
The second thing to
understand is how

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.960
to translate metrics to volume.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.960
The key to that is to use
a rough rule of thumb,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.770
to treat a single time series
data point as 8 bytes of data.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.490
So if we sample a single
time series every minute

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.130
for a month, that'll
generate about one third

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.780
of a megabyte of data.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.630
You will then be charged based
on the total volume of data

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.410
collected for all of
the metrics that are

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.290
being collected in the project.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.430
Now that we understand
how monitoring is priced,

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.270
we need to see how much
data we're actually

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.720
ingesting and storing.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.300
And we can do that
in two places--

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.780
in the Billing Console
and in Stackdriver itself.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.923
First, let's start with
the Billing Console.

00:01:38.923 --> 00:01:40.590
You can access it
from the Cloud Console

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.320
by going to the Products menu,
selecting Billing, and then

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.270
going to Reports.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.640
From there, you can search
through just the costs

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.950
associated with the
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.810
and narrow the report
to the projects you

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.305
are responsible for managing.

00:01:53.305 --> 00:01:54.680
This is a great
way to understand

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.850
your historical usage and costs.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.220
You can also see usage
information in the Stackdriver

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.440
Workspace by going to
the Workspace settings

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.480
and selecting Usage details.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.920
Here, you can see
exactly how much

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.740
metric data is being ingested
from every project that

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.620
is being monitored
by the Workspace.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.200
Finally, metrics that show
how much monitoring data is

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.983
being ingested are available
in Stackdriver itself.

00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:18.150
You can use them to do
things like create dashboards

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.300
and alert on unexpected
spikes in monitoring data.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.040
Now you know where
your monitoring costs

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.240
are coming from.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.520
But what can you do to
actually manage that volume

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.215
or even reduce it?

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:30.090
The first thing to look
at may be your custom

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.860
metric instrumentation.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.720
Frameworks like
OpenCensus and Prometheus

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.330
generate a lot of metrics when
you first start using them.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.700
You may not need all
of that information.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.410
For example, the
node exporter will

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.285
provide the same
information you were getting

00:02:42.285 --> 00:02:44.128
from GKE node monitoring.

00:02:44.128 --> 00:02:46.170
Review the work you've
done to add custom metrics

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.360
to your service, and
determine whether all of them

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.500
are really necessary.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.370
The second thing you should
review is your use of labels.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.620
The way that you use
labels for GCP components

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.630
might impact the
volume of time series

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.360
that are generated for your
metrics and monitoring.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.760
For example, you can
use labels on your VMs

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.880
to appropriately report metrics
to cost centers on your GCP

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.720
bill and to signify whether
specific GCP environments are

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.970
production or development.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.670
But adding these labels means
that additional time series

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.205
are generated in monitoring.

00:03:16.205 --> 00:03:18.330
If you label your virtual
machines with cost center

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.110
and {} environment values, then
you can calculate the total

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.420
number of time series by
multiplying the cardinality

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.200
of both labels.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.980
So if there are 11 cost center
values and five environment

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.485
values, that means that 55
time series are generated.

00:03:32.485 --> 00:03:34.110
This is why the way
that you add labels

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.360
might add significant
metric volume

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.333
and therefore increase the cost.

00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:39.750
Of course, labels
are helpful when

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.620
you're looking to
organize your resources,

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.150
but you may be able to reduce
your complexity and cost.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.420
Finally, you can look at your
monitoring agent configuration.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.700
Metrics sent from the monitoring
agent are chargeable metrics.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.670
The monitoring agent streams
application and system metrics

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.380
from common third-party
applications

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.860
and additional GCP virtual
machine-level metrics.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.440
If you don't need the detailed
system metrics or metrics

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.200
from the third-party apps
for certain virtual machines,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.410
you can reduce the volume by
not sending these metrics.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.150
You can also reduce
the metric volumes

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.005
by reducing the number of VMs
using the monitoring agent.

00:04:15.005 --> 00:04:17.130
Now that we've covered how
to understand and manage

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.529
our monitoring costs,
let's move on to logging.

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:21.810
Let's review logging pricing.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.450
The model here is
very straightforward.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.070
You are charged
based on the amount

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.930
of volume data
ingested in the project

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.760
over the free threshold.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.410
You should know that
unlike monitoring,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.870
where some metrics do
not incur any charges,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.600
all logs ingested incur charges.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.570
Next, let's move on to
reviewing the logging charges.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.340
As with monitoring,
this information's

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.040
available on the Billing
Console, the Cloud Console,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.690
and in Stackdriver itself.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.008
In the Billing Console, you
can filter the data to just

00:04:48.008 --> 00:04:49.800
report on Stackdriver
logging to understand

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.130
the exact costs being
generated by each project.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.260
To better understand why
a particular project might

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.300
be generating high
logging charges,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.670
you can see detailed
information under Log Ingestion

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.650
in the Logging menu.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.720
This view provides a
service-level breakdown

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.300
of exactly where the logging
charges in your project

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.680
are coming from.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.597
To get even more
detailed information,

00:05:09.597 --> 00:05:11.430
you can select the View
and Metrics explorer

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.980
for any row to get a
historical view of how

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.080
these resources were
generating log data over time.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.710
You can then use this view to
create charts and dashboards

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.420
to easily access this
information in the future.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.020
Now that you understand how to
capture, measure, and analyze

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.360
your logging costs, let's
dive into some options

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.980
for managing them.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.530
Our primary mechanism to
control log congestion

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.270
is log exclusions.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.150
They allow you to either
broadly or precisely specify

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.160
exactly which logs
will or will not

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.170
make it into Stackdriver
Logging and be

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.970
available in the Log Viewer.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.550
However, you should remember
that logs aren't just discarded

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:45.780
when they're excluded.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.690
You still have the option
of configuring log exports

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.300
to have them be sent to
cloud storage for archival,

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.030
BigQuery for analysis,
or Pub/Sub for delivery

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.690
to subscribers, even if
they're subject to exclusions.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.705
Exclusions and
exports work together,

00:05:59.705 --> 00:06:01.080
letting you choose
which logs you

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.180
want to keep for
real-time visibility

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.430
and which logs you want to
archive without incurring

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.380
logging costs.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.060
The more complex
your applications,

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.340
the more monitoring and
logging you'll need.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.190
Knowing how these are priced
and how they can be configured

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.438
can help you manage
the resulting costs.

00:06:16.438 --> 00:06:17.730
Thank you for joining me today.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.320
And come back soon for more.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.730
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:21.780
Stay healthy out there.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

